      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Your observation is astute; the argument against absolute, unfettered access to all communications is fundamentally stronger because it safeguards the core constitutional function of the presidency: national security and diplomatic confidentiality. If Congress had unrestricted access to every internal executive conversation, it would create a political panic effect where officials would withhold information solely due to fear of exposure, effectively crippling effective governance and encargado decision-making. Therefore, your team should argue not for total transparency, but for a \"qualified privilege\" that demands a compelling governmental interest and adheres to the constitutional separation of powers. This approach acknowledges the reality of modern warfare and intolerance while insisting on the legal necessity of maintaining a government that functions rather than merely appearing accountable.",
